      <title>Integrating cervical and breast cancer prevention into routine women’s health services in Burkina Faso</title>
      <scientificTitle>Integrated prevention and early detection of cervical and breast cancers within sexual, reproductive, maternal, newborn, child and adolescent health services in Burkina Faso: a multiphase research protocol</scientificTitle>
      <acronym/>
      <studyHypothesis>1. To assess the effect of the integrated model on indicators of cervical and breast cancer prevention and early detection, including cervical cancer screening coverage and early detection of breast abnormalities among eligible women.
2. To assess the effect of the integrated model on the use of women’s contacts with the health system as opportunities for cervical and breast cancer prevention and early detection, including the reduction of missed opportunities and the integration of cervical cancer screening and breast abnormality detection into routine services.
3. To assess women’s satisfaction and acceptability of the integrated model, particularly their satisfaction with receiving multiple integrated health services, including cervical cancer screening and early detection of breast abnormalities, during the same contact with health services.</studyHypothesis>
      <plainEnglishSummary>Background and study aims
Cervical cancer and breast cancer are the two most frequent and most deadly cancers among women in Burkina Faso. Simple and effective ways of detecting them early do exist, but very few women benefit from them: about one woman in four has ever been screened for cervical cancer and about one in seven for breast cancer, whereas the globally recommended target is 70%. One reason is that these services are usually offered separately, which forces women to make repeated trips and visits. Yet sexual, reproductive, maternal, newborn, child and adolescent health (SRMNCAH) services, such as antenatal care, family planning or childhood immunization, already see women on a regular basis.
The study therefore aims to design, together with frontline stakeholders, and then test a model that integrates screening for both cancers directly into these routine services. The purpose is to improve access, coverage, quality and continuity of prevention, and to generate evidence useful for national decisions and for a possible scale-up of the model.

Who can participate? 
Recruitment takes place at three levels:
1. At the institutional level, first-level health and social promotion centers (CSPS) offering SRMNCAH services and having a minimum capacity, namely counseling, cervical cancer screening (VIA/VILI), clinical breast examination and functioning thermocoagulation or cryotherapy, together with their staff (nurses, midwives, doctors, health facility managers). Facilities already involved in a similar intervention, or lacking the required minimum capacity, are excluded.
2. At the beneficiary level, women aged 25 to 65 years attending SRMNCAH services in the included CSPS, excluding those with a history of total hysterectomy or bilateral mastectomy.
3. At the community level, community-based health workers, community organizations, volunteers and women taking part in community activities. Participation is conditional on free and informed written consent, available in French and in local languages, with the possibility of withdrawing at any time without consequence.

What does the study involve? 
Four CSPS are all in the control group at the start of the study, after which one CSPS switches to the intervention at the start of each of the four following periods. The intervention is an integrated model of cervical and breast cancer prevention within SRMNCAH services, built around four dimensions, namely professional integration (training, mentoring, supervision), organizational integration (embedding screening in family planning, antenatal and postnatal care, immunization and child health), clinical integration (VIA/VILI, clinical breast examination, same-day treatment of eligible precancerous lesions, referral) and community integration (awareness raising, social mobilization, referral by community workers). The data are mixed, with repeated interviews and focus groups on one hand and data monitoring on the other, complemented by three participatory feedback workshops.

What are the possible benefits and risks of participating? 
The risks are considered low. They concern discomfort during screening, anxiety linked to a positive result and, for women treated on the same day for a precancerous cervical lesion, the risks specific to thermocoagulation or cryotherapy, namely pain, vaginal discharge, bleeding and, rarely, infection. These risks are mitigated by staff training, quality assurance procedures and an appropriate referral system. The expected benefits are earlier detection of cervical and breast cancer and better access to services. The study design ensures that all participating health facilities will benefit from the intervention.

Where is the study run from?
Joseph Ki-Zerbo University (Burkina Faso)

When is the study starting and how long is it expected to run for? 
October 2026 to September 2027

Who is funding the study? 
The OR Tambo Africa Research Chair, Research Chair for Action Against Cancer in Africa (ReAAC), hosted at Joseph Ki-Zerbo University (Burkina Faso)

Who is the main contact? 
Mr Lankoandé Larba, lankoandefrere@gmail.com</plainEnglishSummary>
