    <interventions>
      <intervention>
	<description>The intervention will be implemented in four Health and Social Promotion Centres (HSPCs) using a stepped-wedge cluster-randomized design. All four HSPCs will initially provide routine care and undergo a baseline assessment. The HSPCs will then be randomly allocated to different intervention sequences, with successive groups of HSPCs crossing over from the control condition to the integrated model at predefined timepoints. By the end of the study, all four HSPCs will have implemented the intervention. The randomization sequence will be generated by a statistician independent of the field team using computer software, which reduces the risk of selection bias.

The integrated model will use women’s routine contacts with health services as opportunities for cervical and breast cancer prevention and early detection, with the aim of reducing missed opportunities. The intervention will be integrated into routine sexual, reproductive, maternal, newborn, child and adolescent health (SRMNCAH) services and will include provider training and mentoring; health education and counselling; cervical cancer screening using VIA/VILI, as applicable; clinical breast examination for the early detection of breast abnormalities; identification and reduction of missed opportunities; referral and follow-up of women with abnormal findings; and strengthening of service organization, coordination, and patient flow to facilitate the delivery of multiple services during the same health-system contact.

Repeated measurements will be conducted across study periods to assess changes associated with the introduction of the integrated model, including cervical cancer screening, early detection of breast abnormalities, missed opportunities, use of integrated services, and women’s satisfaction.</description>
