    <interventions>
      <intervention>
	<description>Participants will be invited to take part in prostate cancer screening as part of the IMProVE study via 1 of 5 invitation routes, selected at random:
1.	SMS invitation only
2.	SMS invitation + SMS reminder
3.	SMS invitation + letter reminder
4.	Letter invitation + SMS reminder
5.	Letter invitation only
These invitation methods will form a Study Within a Trial (SWAT). A SWAT analysis will be conducted after the first 10,000 invitations to evaluate the effectiveness of each methods, with ineffective methods discontinued.

Participants will be identified by NHS DigiTrials via searches of the Personal Demographics Service (PDS). Searches will be based on predefined postcode sectors within Sheffield and Leeds. A self-referral pathway will also be available to participants outside the main 5 invitation routes.

Consenting participants will be invited to mobile clinic visit in the community, where they will undergo Prostate Specific Antigen (PSA) and optional Polygenic Risk Scoring (PRS) testing. Participants who complete the PSA test will then be randomised into one of two study arms. Randomisation will be conducted by the Central Research Nursing Team using a randomisation electronic Case Report Form (eCRF) hosted in REDCap after valid PSA results are received from the laboratory. The randomisation schedule will be generated and maintained by the Trial Statistician. Randomisation will occur after PSA sampling, but the PSA result will not influence group allocation:
•	Image-guided pathway:  Participants whose PSA is &gt;1ng/ml will be referred for a bi-parametric MRI (bpMRI) within a Community Diagnostic Centre (CDC). Participants found to have a lesion with a Likert score 4 or 5, will be referred for an image-guided prostate biopsy within a CDC. Based on the biopsy and MRI results, participants will either be discharged, referred for surveillance or referred for radical treatment via their local secondary care provider. Those with a Likert score of 3 will be offered a repeat PSA test at 6 months following their initial PSA result date. Those found to have a PSA density &gt; 0.15nf/ml2 on the repeat PSA, will be referred to their local secondary care provider for a prostate biopsy as per local practice.
•	NHS Pathway: Participants whose PSA is above their age-specific threshold will be referred for a multi-parametric MRI (mpMRI) within a CDC. Participants found to have a lesion with a Likert score of 4 or 5, and those with Likert 3 with a PSA density ≥0.15ng/ml2 will be referred for a prostate biopsy within a CDC. Based on the biopsy and MRI results, participants will either be discharged, referred for surveillance or referred for radical treatment via their local secondary care provider.</description>
